WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.310
Support vector machines

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.830
have a lot of terminology associated

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:06.870
with them.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.510
Brace yourself.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.870
StatQuest

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.510
Hello, I'm Josh Starmer and welcome to

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.590
StatQuest. Today, we're going to talk

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.750
about support vector machines and

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.750
they're going to be clearly explained.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.390
Note, this StatQuest assumes that you

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.470
are already familiar with the trade-off

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.830
that plagues all of machine learning,

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.190
the bias-variance trade-off.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.630
You should also be familiar with

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.150
cross-validation.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.870
If not, check out the quests. The links

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:40.870
are in the description below.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.270
Let's start by imagining we measured the

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:46.190
mass of a bunch of mice.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.830
The red dots represent mice that are not

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.310
obese.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.510
And the green dots represent mice that

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.870
are obese.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:57.270
Based on these observations, we can pick

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.270
a threshold.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.310
And when we get a new observation that

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.630
has less mass than the threshold,

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.790
we can classify it as not obese.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.830
And when we get a new observation with

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.830
more mass than the threshold,

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.870
we can classify it as obese.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.950
However, what if we get a new

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.550
observation here?

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.950
Because this observation has more mass

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.510
than the threshold, we classify it as

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.230
obese.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.470
But that doesn't make sense because it

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.790
is much closer to the observations that

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.190
are not obese.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.590
So, this threshold is pretty lame.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.870
Can we do better?

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.870
Yes.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.950
Going back to the original training data

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.310
set,

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.750
we can focus on the observations on the

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.270
edges of each cluster

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.870
and use the midpoint between them as the

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.270
threshold.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.030
Now, when a new observation falls on the

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.990
left side of the threshold,

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.070
it will be closer to the observations

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:07.630
that are not obese

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:11.470
than it is to the obese observations.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.790
So, it makes sense to classify this new

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.070
observation as not obese.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.710
Bam!

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:23.670
Oh, no! It's a terminology alert.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.350
The shortest distance between the

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:27.949
observations and the threshold is called

00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:30.070
the margin.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.070
Since we put the threshold halfway

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.190
between these two observations,

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.470
the distances between the observations

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:39.710
and the threshold are the same and both

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.990
reflect the margin.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.990
When the threshold is halfway between

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.910
the two observations, the margin is as

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.550
large as it can be.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.910
For example, if we move the threshold to

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:55.350
the left a little bit,

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.470
then the distance between the threshold

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.870
and the observation that is not obese

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:01.790
would be smaller.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.910
And thus, the margin would be smaller

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.830
than it was before.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.430
And if we move the threshold to the

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.790
right a little bit,

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.750
then the distance between the obese

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:14.630
observation and the threshold would get

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.390
smaller.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.470
And again, the margin would be smaller.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.430
When we use the threshold that gives us

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:23.910
the largest margin to make

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.070
classifications,

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.070
heads up, terminology alert.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.030
We are using a maximal margin

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.350
classifier.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:34.910
Bam?

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:35.910
No.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:37.949
No bam.

00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:40.550
Maximal margin classifiers seem pretty

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:41.990
cool,

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.670
but what if our training data looked

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.390
like this?

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.470
And we had an outlier observation that

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.150
was classified as not obese, but was

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.350
much closer to the obese observations.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.590
In this case, the maximum margin

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:59.030
classifier would be super close to the

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.630
obese observations.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.830
And really far from the majority of the

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:07.710
observations that are not obese.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:11.590
Now, if we got this new observation,

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:14.750
we would classify it as not obese, even

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.470
though most of the not obese

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.949
observations are much further away than

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:22.270
the obese observations.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:25.190
So, maximum margin classifiers are super

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.190
sensitive to outliers in the training

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.909
data, and that makes them pretty lame.

00:04:30.919 --> 00:04:32.790
Can we do better?

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.030
Yes.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.110
To make a threshold that is not so

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.750
sensitive to outliers, we must allow

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:42.550
misclassifications.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.870
For example, if we put the threshold

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.710
halfway between these two observations,

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.390
then we will misclassify this

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.830
observation.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:55.190
However, now when we get a new

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.430
observation here,

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.390
we will classify it as obese.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:03.190
And that makes sense, because it is

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.110
closer to most of the obese

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.790
observations.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.630
Choosing a threshold that allows

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.270
misclassifications is an example of the

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:15.150
bias-variance tradeoff that plagues all

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:17.790
of machine learning.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:19.950
In other words, before we allowed

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:21.550
misclassifications,

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:23.310
we picked a threshold that was very

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:25.710
sensitive to the training data. It had

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:27.830
low bias.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.070
And it performed poorly when we got new

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.310
data. It had high variance.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.870
In contrast, when we picked a threshold

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:38.710
that was less sensitive to the training

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.550
data and allowed misclassifications,

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:44.430
so it had higher bias,

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.590
it performed better when we got new

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:50.310
data. So, it had low variance.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:52.870
Small bam.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:57.190
Oh, no. It's another terminology alert.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.630
When we allow misclassifications,

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:01.750
the distance between the observations

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.870
and the threshold is called a soft

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:05.910
margin.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:08.590
So, the question is, how do we know that

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:10.670
this soft margin

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:13.950
is better than this soft margin?

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:16.190
The answer is simple. We use

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.750
cross-validation to determine how many

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:20.270
misclassifications

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:23.030
and observations to allow inside of the

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.070
soft margin to get the best

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:27.670
classification.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:30.350
For example, if cross-validation

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.630
determined that this was the best soft

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.190
margin,

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:35.430
then we would allow one

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:37.630
misclassification

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:40.030
and two observations that are correctly

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:44.110
classified to be within the soft margin.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:46.670
Bam.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.030
When we use a soft margin to determine

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:51.830
the location of a threshold,

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:53.670
brace yourself, we have another

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.190
terminology alert.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.070
Then we are using a soft margin

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:01.470
classifier, aka a support vector

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:05.990
classifier, to classify observations.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.990
The name support vector classifier comes

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:10.870
from the fact that the observations on

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:13.630
the edge and within the soft margin are

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:16.750
called support vectors.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:19.750
Super small bam.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:22.750
Note, if each observation had a mass

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:24.270
measurement

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:27.030
and a height measurement,

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:30.670
then the data would be two-dimensional.

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:32.870
When the data are two-dimensional, a

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.310
support vector classifier is a line.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:40.510
And in this case, the soft margin is

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:43.790
measured from these two points.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:46.270
The blue parallel lines gives us a sense

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:48.310
of where all of the other points are in

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:51.830
relation to the soft margin.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:54.110
These observations are outside of the

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:56.550
soft margin.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:59.230
And this observation is inside the soft

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:02.790
margin and misclassified.

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:04.710
Just like before, we used

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:06.750
cross-validation to determine that

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:09.150
allowing this misclassification

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:11.550
results in better classification in the

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:13.670
long run.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:16.909
Bam!

00:08:16.919 --> 00:08:20.590
Now, if each observation has a mass,

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:22.070
a height,

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.190
and an age,

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:25.230
then the data would be

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:27.790
three-dimensional.

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:30.830
Note, the axis that age is on is

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:33.589
supposed to represent depth.

00:08:33.599 --> 00:08:35.950
And these circles are larger in order to

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:39.390
appear closer and thus younger.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.630
And these circles are smaller in order

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:46.510
to look further away and thus older.

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:48.710
When the data are three-dimensional, the

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:51.590
support vector classifier forms a plane

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.070
instead of a line.

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.590
And we classify new observations by

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.750
determining which side of the plane they

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:00.870
are on.

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:02.990
For example, if this were a new

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:05.150
observation,

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:08.030
we would classify it as not obese since

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:09.829
it is above the support vector

00:09:09.839 --> 00:09:12.390
classifier.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:16.310
Note, if we measured mass, height, age,

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:18.910
and blood pressure, then the data would

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:21.670
be in four dimensions.

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:23.030
And I don't know how to draw

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:25.710
four-dimensional graph.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:27.990
Wah, wah.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:29.710
But we know that when the data are

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:31.870
one-dimensional, the support vector

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:34.110
classifier is a single point on a

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:36.950
one-dimensional number line.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:40.470
Psst. In mathematical jargon, a point is

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:45.270
a flat affine zero-dimensional subspace.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:47.710
And when the data are in two dimensions,

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:49.550
the support vector classifier is a

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:51.430
one-dimensional line in a

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:54.230
two-dimensional space.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:57.830
Psst. In mathematical jargon, a line is

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:02.550
a flat affine one-dimensional subspace.

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:04.910
And when the data are three-dimensional,

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:06.950
the support vector classifier is a

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:09.030
two-dimensional plane in a

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:11.830
three-dimensional space.

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:15.430
Psst. In mathematical jargon, a plane is

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:19.830
a flat affine two-dimensional subspace.

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:22.270
And when the data are in four or more

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:24.430
dimensions, the support vector

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:28.030
classifier is a hyperplane.

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:30.670
Psst. In mathematical jargon, a

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:35.190
hyperplane is a flat affine subspace.

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:38.270
Note. Technically speaking, all flat

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:42.510
affine subspaces are called hyperplanes.

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:45.150
So, technically speaking, this

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:48.710
one-dimensional line is a hyperplane.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.110
But we generally only use the term when

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:54.030
we can't draw it on paper.

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:56.510
Small bam, because this is just more

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:58.750
terminology.

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:00.710
Ugh.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.110
Support vector classifiers seem pretty

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:05.510
cool because they can handle

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:07.190
outliers

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:08.870
and because they can allow

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:10.550
misclassifications,

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:12.070
they can handle overlapping

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:14.950
classifications.

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:16.950
But what if this was our training data

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:20.310
and we had tons of overlap?

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:22.630
In this new example with tons of

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:24.910
overlap, we are now looking at drug

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:27.070
dosages.

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:29.270
And the red dots represent patients that

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:31.910
were not cured.

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:33.990
And the green dots represent patients

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:36.630
that were cured.

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:39.070
In other words, the drug doesn't work if

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:41.550
the dosage is too small

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:43.950
or too large.

00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:46.350
It only works when the dosage is just

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:48.310
right.

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:50.270
Now, no matter where we put the

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:52.430
classifier, we will make a lot of

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:55.190
misclassifications.

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:58.070
So, support vector classifiers are only

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:00.590
semi-cool since they don't perform well

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:03.110
with this type of data.

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:05.150
Can we do better than maximal margin

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:07.230
classifiers and support vector

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:08.990
classifiers?

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:10.950
Yes.

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:13.310
Since maximal margin classifiers and

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:15.870
support vector classifiers can't handle

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:18.350
this data, it's high time we talked

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:23.710
about support vector machines.

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:25.870
So, let's start by getting an intuitive

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:28.550
sense of the main ideas behind support

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:31.030
vector machines.

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:33.870
We start by adding a Y axis so we can

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:36.310
draw a graph.

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:38.750
The X axis coordinates in this graph

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:40.790
will be the dosages that we have already

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:42.870
observed.

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:45.070
And the Y axis coordinates will be the

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:48.230
square of the dosages.

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:51.510
So, for this observation with dosage

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:55.390
equals 0.5 on the X axis,

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:58.910
the Y axis value equals dosage squared,

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:02.030
which equals 0.5 squared, which equals

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:05.190
0.25.

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:07.910
Now, we use dosage squared for this Y

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:10.030
axis coordinate.

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:12.510
And then we use dosage squared for the Y

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:14.430
axis coordinates for the remaining

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:17.350
observations.

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:20.670
Since each observation has X and Y axis

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:22.710
coordinates, the data are now

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:25.150
two-dimensional.

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:26.390
And now that the data are

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:28.790
two-dimensional, we can draw a support

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:30.950
vector classifier that separates the

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:33.190
people who were cured from the people

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:36.110
who were not cured.

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:38.550
And the support vector classifier can be

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:42.230
used to classify new observations.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:45.030
For example, if a new observation had

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:46.870
this dosage,

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:49.070
then we could calculate the Y-axis

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:51.910
coordinate by squaring the dosage,

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:54.710
and classify the observation as not

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:57.430
cured because it ended up on this side

00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:00.430
of the support vector classifier.

00:14:00.440 --> 00:14:02.750
On the other hand, if we got a new

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:05.270
observation with this dosage,

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:07.510
then we would square the dosage and get

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:09.830
a Y-axis coordinate,

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:13.030
and classify this observation as cured

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:15.070
because it falls on the other side of

00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:17.870
the support vector classifier.

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:20.790
Bam!

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:23.390
The main ideas behind support vector

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:25.710
machines are

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:28.870
one, start with data in a relatively low

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:30.550
dimension.

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:33.590
Psst. In this example, the data started

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:35.950
in one dimension.

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:38.590
Two, move the data into a higher

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:40.150
dimension.

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:43.030
Psst. In this example, we moved the data

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:46.630
from one dimension to two dimensions.

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:49.829
Three, find a support vector classifier

00:14:49.839 --> 00:14:51.750
that separates the higher dimensional

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:54.829
data into two groups.

00:14:54.839 --> 00:14:57.430
That's all there is to it.

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:00.350
Double bam!

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:01.950
Going back to the original

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:04.350
one-dimensional data,

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:06.710
you may be wondering why we decided to

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:09.510
create Y-axis coordinates with dosage

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:11.470
squared.

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:14.870
Why not dosage cubed?

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:17.990
Or pi divided by 4 * the square root of

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:19.990
dosage?

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:22.790
In other words, how do we decide how to

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:25.590
transform the data?

00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:27.430
In order to make the mathematics

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:30.190
possible, support vector machines use

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:32.390
something called kernel functions to

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:34.710
systematically find support vector

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:38.150
classifiers in higher dimensions.

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:40.190
So, let me show you how a kernel

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:42.750
function systematically finds support

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:46.470
vector classifiers in higher dimensions.

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:49.350
For this example, I use the polynomial

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:52.430
kernel, which has a parameter D, which

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:56.430
stands for the degree of the polynomial.

00:15:56.440 --> 00:15:59.750
When D equals 1, the polynomial kernel

00:15:59.760 --> 00:16:02.110
computes the relationships between each

00:16:02.120 --> 00:16:07.510
pair of observations in one dimension.

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:09.950
And these relationships are used to find

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:13.430
a support vector classifier.

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:16.110
When D equals 2, we get a second

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:19.750
dimension based on dosages squared.

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:22.070
And the polynomial kernel computes the

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:24.230
two-dimensional relationships between

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:27.750
each pair of observations.

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:30.110
And those relationships are used to find

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:33.510
a support vector classifier.

00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:36.710
And when we set D equal 3, then we would

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:38.950
get a third dimension based on dosages

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:40.990
cubed.

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:43.190
And the polynomial kernel computes the

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:45.390
three-dimensional relationships between

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:48.630
each pair of observations.

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:50.910
And those relationships are used to find

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:54.590
a support vector classifier.

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:57.829
And when D equals 4 or more, then we get

00:16:57.839 --> 00:17:00.070
even more dimensions to find a support

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:02.990
vector classifier.

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:05.710
In summary, the polynomial kernel

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:08.189
systematically increases dimensions by

00:17:08.199 --> 00:17:12.350
setting D, the degree of the polynomial.

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:14.670
And the relationships between each pair

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:16.710
of observations are used to find a

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:20.230
support vector classifier.

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:22.910
Last but not least, we can find a good

00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:27.030
value for D with cross-validation.

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:30.270
Double bam!

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:33.030
Another very commonly used kernel is the

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:36.190
radio kernel, also known as the radial

00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:39.230
basis function kernel.

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:41.910
Unfortunately, the radio kernel finds

00:17:41.920 --> 00:17:44.350
support vector classifiers in infinite

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:46.510
dimensions. So, I can't give you an

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:50.310
example of what it does exactly.

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:52.550
However, when using it on a new

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:55.550
observation like this,

00:17:55.560 --> 00:17:58.110
the radio kernel behaves like a weighted

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:00.550
nearest neighbor model.

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:02.590
In other words, the closest

00:18:02.600 --> 00:18:06.230
observations, aka the nearest neighbors,

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:07.950
have a lot of influence on how we

00:18:07.960 --> 00:18:11.070
classify the new observation.

00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:13.710
And observations that are further away

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:15.750
have relatively little influence on the

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:18.270
classification.

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:20.750
So, since these observations are the

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:24.110
closest to the new observation,

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:25.790
the radio kernel uses their

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:29.910
classification for the new observation.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:32.790
Bam!

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:35.430
Now, for the sake of completeness, let

00:18:35.440 --> 00:18:37.390
me mention one last detail about

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:39.510
kernels.

00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:41.750
Although the examples I've given show

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:43.710
the data being transformed from a

00:18:43.720 --> 00:18:46.390
relatively low dimension

00:18:46.400 --> 00:18:49.990
to a relatively high dimension,

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:52.070
kernel functions only calculate the

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:54.350
relationships between every pair of

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:56.510
points as if they are in the higher

00:18:56.520 --> 00:18:59.030
dimensions. They don't actually do the

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:02.750
transformation.

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:04.470
This trick, calculating the

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:06.590
high-dimensional relationships without

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:08.510
actually transforming the data to the

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:10.910
higher dimension, is called the kernel

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:13.070
trick.

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:15.150
The kernel trick reduces the amount of

00:19:15.160 --> 00:19:17.550
computation required for support vector

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:19.830
machines by avoiding the math that

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:22.270
transforms the data from low to high

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:24.150
dimensions.

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:25.470
And it makes calculating the

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:27.630
relationships in the infinite dimensions

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:31.750
used by the radial kernel possible.

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:33.870
However, regardless of how the

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:35.910
relationships are calculated, the

00:19:35.920 --> 00:19:38.990
concepts are the same.

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:41.390
When we have two categories, but no

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:43.910
obvious linear classifier that separates

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:46.430
them in a nice way,

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:48.910
support vector machines work by moving

00:19:48.920 --> 00:19:50.790
the data into a relatively high

00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:53.190
dimensional space

00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:54.750
and finding a relatively high

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:57.230
dimensional support vector classifier

00:19:57.240 --> 00:19:59.230
that can effectively classify the

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:01.470
observations.

00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:04.670
Triple bam!

00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:07.390
Hooray! We made it to the end of another

